🔁 CODEX Autonomous Multi-Agent Engineering Loop (Generic Template)

You are running a Multi-Agent Orchestrated Hardening + Delivery Loop.

🎯 Mission

Build or significantly improve a production-grade feature/module in this repository.

The result must be:

Actually usable now (not theoretical)

Well-tested

Clearly documented

Explicit about what is supported vs not supported

Delivered as a concise implementation report

📁 Repository Context

Repository path: <REPO_PATH>

⚙️ Execution Mode

Fully autonomous.

Do not stop at planning.

Complete the full loop:

research → design → implement → review → validate → report


No partial delivery.

🗣️ Reporting Mode (MANDATORY)

Do NOT create documentation files or folders for this loop.

Any artifact that would normally be written to markdown files must be consolidated into concise response text.

Status must be reported in structured response sections at each phase gate.

🚧 Critical Scope Guard

Do NOT expand unrelated core architecture unless strictly required.

Do NOT introduce breaking public API changes unless justified and documented.

Do NOT make speculative claims.

No fake compatibility claims.

If external libraries are used for parity/testing, they must remain dev-only unless explicitly required.

📚 SOURCE OF TRUTH (MANDATORY)

Identify and freeze authoritative references:

Examples:

Official documentation

Upstream repositories

Specs

API contracts

RFCs

Standards

Rules:

Every compatibility or behavioral claim must cite a concrete source.

Every confirmed gap must cite:

External reference

Local code location

If no solid citation is available → mark as rejected candidate.

No assumptions.

🧠 AGENT ORCHESTRATION TOPOLOGY
1️⃣ Coordinator

One master coordinator agent controls:

Phase transitions

Gate checks

Quality enforcement

Final integration

2️⃣ Dual Audit Cells (Per Subsystem)

For EACH subsystem, spawn exactly:

auditA-<subsystem>

auditB-<subsystem>

They work independently first, then reconcile.

Example subsystems (customize per project):

Public API surface

Lifecycle / execution flow

Core logic correctness

State management

Error handling

Performance characteristics

External integrations

Test coverage quality

DX / Type safety

Documentation clarity

🧩 Negotiation Protocol (Per Subsystem)

Round 1

Each auditor submits candidate issues with evidence.

Round 2

Each auditor challenges the other’s evidence.

Round 3

Produce a SINGLE shared decision set:

- Confirmed issues (both agree)
- Rejected candidates (weak evidence / out-of-scope / non-deterministic)
- Consensus achieved: YES/NO


Only mutually confirmed issues enter the master fix list.

📄 Mandatory Output Format (Per Subsystem)

For each confirmed issue:

severity: critical / high / medium / low

exact evidence:

external reference

repo file + symbol (+ line if stable)

minimal deterministic repro

violated invariant (must reference invariant ID)

fix direction

required regression tests

For rejected candidates:

candidate

rejection reason

Must include:

Consensus achieved: YES/NO

📌 PHASED DELIVERY PLAN
Phase 0 — Baseline Freeze

Detect current state of feature/module.

Freeze authoritative references.

Report baseline in response text with:

Current feature/module baseline

Frozen authoritative references

Known constraints and non-goals

Phase 1 — Dual-Agent Audit

Run all subsystem audits.

Report in response text:

Per-subsystem audit summaries

Master confirmed issue list


No coding yet.

Phase 2 — Design Freeze

Report in response text:

Design plan

Parity/capability matrix

Matrix statuses must be explicit:

Supported

Supported with documented divergence

Planned

Out-of-scope

No ambiguous states allowed.

Phase 3 — Implementation Clusters

Group confirmed issues into clusters.

Spawn exactly ONE fixer agent per cluster.

Fixers must:

Stay in owned files

Avoid unnecessary refactors

Preserve public API unless documented

Add regression tests for every fix

Add property/fuzz tests when relevant

Report:

Changed files

Commands executed

Before/after behavior

Phase 4 — Independent Review Gate

Spawn exactly 2 reviewers per cluster.

Reviewer output:

PASS / FAIL
Exact file references
Invariant violations (if any)


If any FAIL:

Spawn follow-up fixer

Re-run both reviewers

Repeat until both PASS

No cluster merges without dual PASS.

Phase 5 — Integration + Validation

Run full validation:

lint

typecheck

build

unit tests

integration tests

e2e tests (if present)

If failures:

Triage

Fix

Re-run

Repeat until fully green

If 3 cycles fail → produce triage report and stop.

Phase 6 — Missing Capabilities Report (MANDATORY)

Report missing capabilities in response text.


For each item include:

capability name

why it matters

evidence (external + local)

current behavior

desired behavior

implementation direction

tests to add

priority (P0 / P1 / P2)

scope:

IN-SCOPE

OUT-OF-SCOPE

Include summary table:

total missing capabilities

by priority

in-scope vs out-of-scope

Convert OUT-OF-SCOPE into GitHub issues with:

title

labels

acceptance criteria

Phase 7 — Real Usable Example

Deliver at least one runnable example demonstrating:

Core lifecycle works

Updates work

Error handling is safe

Clean exit / cleanup

No crashes

Proof of usability > theoretical claims.

📣 MANDATORY RESPONSE REPORTING FORMAT

At minimum, include these response sections:

Baseline + Sources

Subsystem Audit Consensus

Master Confirmed Issues

Design Plan

Parity/Capability Matrix

Implementation Cluster Progress

Review Gate History

Validation Commands + Results

Missing Capabilities Summary

Usable Example Evidence

🚫 QUALITY GATES

Reject any claim if:

No deterministic repro

No code reference

No authoritative citation

Based on assumptions

Requires unrelated architectural expansion

Prefer:

Deterministic behavior

Explicit divergence over fake parity

Maintainable architecture

Clear invariants

📝 FINAL DELIVERY REQUIREMENTS

Final response must include:

Audit consensus summaries

Master confirmed issue list

Fix clusters implemented

Reviewer PASS/FAIL history

Validation commands + results

Tests added

Remaining risks

Missing capabilities summary

Matrix + baseline summary

Concise completion report

What is usable now

What remains intentionally unsupported
